<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="free phenylpropanoid acid biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-2181" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The pathway depicted here is believed of less significance &lt;i&gt;in planta&lt;/i&gt;. The formation of hydroxycinnamic acids, at least ferulic acid and sinapic acid has been demonstrated not to occur via the free phenylpropanoid acids (this pathway) but via the metabolic sequence described in the ferulate amd sinapate biosynthesis (|FRAME:PWY-5168|). However, the existence of this pathway cannot entirely ruled out since mutants defective in the key enzyme presumably generating ferulic acid and sinapic acid |CITS: [14729911]| still possess small amounts of corresponding hydroxycinnamic acid esters. The free phenylpropanoid acid biosynthesis pathway generates simple hydroxycinnamic acids, containing the haracteristic C6C3 phenylpropane skeleton, as found in cinnamic acids (cinnamic acid, caffeic acid, sinapic acid) and their descendants in the lignin biosynthetic route, coumaryl alcohol, coniferyl alcohol, and sinapyl alcohol. Moreover, the phenylpropanoid pathway is the origin of more complex secondary compounds such as flavonoids, isoflavonoids and stilbenes, formed by condensation with phenylpropanoids and malonyl coenzyme A |CITS: [Dixon02]|. More compound classes derived from phenylpropanoids include sinapate esters, condendsed tannins, anthocyanins, coumarins, and benzoic acids. Some phenylpropanoids such as lignins, hydroxycinnamic acids and flavonoids are ubiquitous distributed in higher plants whereas other classes, like the isoflavonoids and stilbenes have a more restricted distribution within the plant kingdom. The functions of phenylpropanoid vary as much as their structural composition. Phenylpropanoids serve as pigments, phytoalexins, phytoanticipins, UV protectants and signal molecules between plant and microbes, they can polymerize and fulfill functions of cell wall stability, provide structures for evapotranspiration (lignin) and protect plants against loss of water (cutin, suberin) |CITS: [Hahlbrock89]|. It has been argued for a long time that the enzymes of the phenylpropanoid pathway are organized in one or more enzyme complexes. Phenylpropanoid metabolism is largly unique to plants, and although quite complex one of the best-studied pathways in plants. There is now evidence for the coordinated expression of genes and enzymes, channeling intermediates into a controlled metabolic flux which triggers the subsequent pathways in a concerted manner |CITS: [15725058]|. Dixon&apos;s group |CITS: [15472080]| could demonstrate the existence of a multi-enzyme complex for the coordinated action of phenylalanine ammonia lyase (PAL) and cinnamate-4-hydroxylase (C4H) controlling the flux of intermediates in the phenylpropanoid biosynthesis.</Description>
